          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"cofield-nikkis-touch-271daac625fd\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":3,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"The first “I love you” directly avows the speaker’s loving state, strongly supporting T. The narration that his voice is “full of promise” gives the avowal substantial relational-undertaking force and therefore supports P, although no explicit commitment is articulated. Murmuring during a kiss and immediately repeating the declaration indicate intimacy and intensity but do not independently establish expressive/reflexive discharge, so E is unsupported.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The degree of P is somewhat uncertain because “full of promise” may describe romantic tone and future potential rather than a definite undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A male character directly says “I love you” to Nikki while kissing her; he then repeats and intensifies the declaration.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The utterance is framed as an intimate, sincere-seeming avowal delivered against Nikki’s lips and reinforced by “I love you so much, Nikki.” The narrator’s description of his voice as “full of promise” presents it not only as a statement of feeling but also as carrying prospective relational commitment. Nothing in the supplied passage indicates irony, deception, coercion, or reflex-like loss of verbal control.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you,” he murmured against her lips\",\"supports\":\"The direct first-person declaration strongly supports T; the intimate delivery supplies context but does not by itself support E.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“his voice full of promise”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit narratorial framing supplies substantial evidence that the declaration invokes a relational or future-oriented undertaking, supporting P.\",\"confidence\":0.88},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you so much, Nikki.”\",\"supports\":\"The immediate personalized repetition reinforces the interpretation of the highlighted utterance as a strong avowal of loving feeling rather than a semantically empty token.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal, while P captures the promise-laden relational force. Delivery and intensity remain contextual features, with no residual core force requiring E or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The classification applies to the first matched “I love you”; the following repetition is used as immediate contextual evidence.\"}"
        }
